\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces The residual TemporalBlock used in every TCN of this thesis: two weight-normalised dilated causal 1-D convolutions, each followed by a ReLU and dropout, summed with a residual connection (kernel size~3).}}{10}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces A single LSTM cell at time step $t$: the forget, input, and output gates regulate how the cell state $C_t$ and hidden state $h_t$ are updated from the previous states $C_{t-1}$, $h_{t-1}$ and the input $x_t$.}}{11}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Engagement class distribution by dataset.}}{16}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The five monolithic baseline architectures. Each applies a \emph {single} encoder to the full feature sequence (or pooled I3D vector) before classification.}}{19}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Semantic-group hybrid architecture. Each OpenFace group is encoded independently into a 64-d embedding (group encoders are chosen per group from TCN / Transformer (T) / LSTM); the I3D clip vector is encoded by an MLP into a 128-d embedding. The six embeddings are concatenated (448-d) and classified by a shared MLP; each stream also feeds an auxiliary head. The OpenFace-only variant drops the I3D row and fuses a 320-d vector.}}{20}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces The three interchangeable per-group encoders. Each maps a group's $T\times D_{\text {group}}$ sequence to a 64-dimensional embedding through temporal mean-pooling and layer normalisation; one is chosen independently for each of the five groups.}}{21}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Rank agreement (Kendall $\tau $) between the six metrics over the fifteen baseline configurations, in-domain on CMOSE (MAE metrics sign-flipped so positive $\tau $ means agreement). The metrics form a micro block and a macro block that barely agree; QWK is the only metric correlated with both.}}{25}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces What each loss trades: the three primary metrics for every baseline across the three losses, in-domain on CMOSE. Macro-accuracy and macro-MAE improve as the loss is rebalanced, while QWK is generally highest under cross-entropy.}}{27}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces The five base models under the three losses on all six metrics, in-domain on CMOSE. The metrics disagree on the winner: accuracy, MAE, and Cohen's kappa favour the I3D MLP under cross-entropy, QWK favours the OpenFace TCN, and the macro metrics peak under the balanced losses.}}{28}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Pairwise prediction agreement (Cohen's $\kappa $) between all fifteen baseline configurations on the in-domain CMOSE test set, in model-major blocks. The diagonal blocks are the same architecture under different losses; agreement is moderate at best everywhere.}}{29}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Cross-domain generalisation, best base model per cell, on the three primary metrics --- QWK, macro-accuracy, and macro-MAE (lower is better) --- plus raw accuracy, the deceptive foil: accuracy stays high in exactly the off-diagonal cells where every chance-corrected metric collapses.}}{30}{figure.caption.23}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Hybrid ablation across all 243 per-group encoder configurations, with and without I3D, on all six metrics (in-domain CMOSE). Each dashed line is the best base model on that metric, across all three losses.}}{34}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Per-class F1: best base versus best hybrid (in-domain CMOSE).}}{36}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Cross-domain generalisation, best hybrid per cell, on the three primary metrics --- QWK, macro-accuracy, and macro-MAE (lower is better) --- plus raw accuracy, which again stays high in the off-diagonal cells where the chance-corrected metrics collapse.}}{37}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Cell-by-cell hybrid advantage: best-hybrid minus best-base QWK over the 3$\times $3 train$\times $test matrix (Figure~\ref {fig:crossdomain_hybrid} minus Figure~\ref {fig:crossdomain_base}). Every cell is positive --- the hybrid wins the whole matrix --- and the gain is largest on the unseen, self-collected Private column. The in-domain CMOSE cell and the Private column (bold) are the two comparisons read in Sections~\ref {sec:error} and~\ref {sec:private-results}.}}{38}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Private-set confusion of the combined-trained best base model versus best hybrid (row-normalised).}}{39}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces In-domain QWK versus mean unseen-target QWK with the hybrid ablation population overlaid on the baseline configurations. Within the hybrid family the correlation is negative ($\rho =-0.31$): more in-domain fit means more corpus fit. The hybrid cloud sits above the baselines throughout --- the architecture lifts the trade-off rather than riding it.}}{40}{figure.caption.32}%
